<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahmud of Ghazni</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/971">971</a>–<a href="/years/1030">1030</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Yamin al-Dawla Mahmud</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahmud of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghazni">Ghazni</a> was ruler of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ghaznavid-empire">Ghaznavid Empire</a> from 998 to 1030, from his capital at Ghazni in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>. He led some seventeen raids into northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, defeating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> Shahis, sacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a> and the temple of Somnath in 1025, and annexing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>. He also took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a>, and his court patronised scholars such as al-Biruni and the poet Ferdowsi.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahmud-of-ghazni.jpg" alt="Mahmud of Ghazni" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1314-15 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mahmud_of_Ghazni,_Ghaznavid_ruler,_conquering_Qasdar_(modern_Khuzdar)_in_India,_miniature_from_the_Jami%CA%BF_al-Tawarikh_of_Rashid_al-Din_Il-Khanid_Tabriz_Ms_Or_20_f.108v.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
